I. INTRODUCTION
Cybersecurity in industrial environments involves protecting interconnected systems, networks, and machinery from unauthorized access, disruption, or manipulation. Modern manufacturing facilities rely on both IT (Information Technology) systems and OT (Operational Technology) systems, each with distinct functions and security requirements. The Purdue Model provides a structured, layered approach to separating these environments using firewalls, segmentation, and controlled communication pathways.
Why OT/ICS Security Matters
The importance of OT/ICS security becomes clear when considering that the operational continuity and physical safety of an industrial organization depend on securing every potential point of entry. This includes not only traditional IT access pathways but also the computer-based interfaces, control systems, and networked equipment that support manufacturing operations.
What This Paper Demonstrates
This paper provides an in-depth examination of the Purdue Model architecture and illustrates how the separation of functional levels—particularly the boundary between IT and OT—forms a critical defensive mechanism against cyber threats. The analysis highlights how layered segmentation reduces lateral movement, limits exposure, and enhances overall system resilience.
What the Reader Will Learn
The reader will gain an understanding of how the Purdue Model can be adapted across different industrial environments, such as Caterpillar, Komatsu, or John Deere, to create an effective cybersecurity defense structure. The paper explains how organizations can tailor the model’s layered approach to meet unique operational requirements while maintaining strong protection against external cyberattacks.
 
