Background
The Purdue Model consists of six levels (0–5), ranging from enterprise systems at the top to physical processes at the bottom. Although originally designed to organize industrial processes, it has become a foundational cybersecurity framework referenced in standards such as NIST SP 800 82 and ISA/IEC 62443.
“The Purdue Model is a framework used to separate and secure Operational Technology (OT) environments from Information Technology (IT) environments in industrial organizations… It is widely referenced in industrial cybersecurity standards and guidance such as NIST SP 800 82 and ISA/IEC 62443.” [1]
Threat Landscape
Industrial environments face numerous cyber threats, including:
•	Ransomware attempting lateral movement
•	Phishing leading to credential theft
•	Remote access abuse
•	Supply chain compromise
•	OT malware (Industroyer, Triton, Stuxnet)
These threats target both IT and OT systems, making segmentation essential.
II. SYSTEM DESIGN
A. Purdue Model Architecture (Levels 0–5)
The Purdue Model organizes industrial systems into hierarchical layers:
•	Level 5 – Enterprise Network: Cloud services, ERP, email, corporate IT.
•	Level 4 – Business Planning & Logistics: MES, scheduling, production planning.
•	Level 3 – Operations & Control: SCADA, historians, supervisory systems.
•	Level 2 – Area Supervisory Control: HMIs, local SCADA nodes.
•	Level 1 – Basic Control: PLCs, RTUs, controllers.
•	Level 0 – Physical Process: Sensors, actuators, robots, machinery.
Data flows upward from Level 0 to Level 5, while commands flow downward. This separation ensures that enterprise systems cannot directly manipulate physical machinery without passing through controlled layers.
B. IT/OT Segmentation and Security Boundaries
Segmentation is the core security principle of the Purdue Model. IT and OT systems must be separated to prevent cyber threats originating in enterprise networks from reaching industrial control systems.
Key segmentation components include:
•	Level 3.5 Industrial DMZ: A buffer zone containing jump servers, patch repositories, and monitoring tools.
•	Firewalls: Enforce strict communication rules between levels.
•	Zero Trust Architecture: Ensures no implicit trust between IT and OT systems.
•	Access Control: Limits who can interact with ICS devices.
•	SOC Tool Mapping:
o	Sentinel → Levels 4–5
o	Defender → IT endpoints
o	Claroty/Nozomi → Levels 0–3
Segmentation prevents lateral movement, ensuring that a compromise in IT does not automatically lead to OT disruption.
C. Industrial Control Systems and Cyber-Physical Components
Industrial Control Systems (ICS) form the core of OT environments. These include:
•	PLCs (Programmable Logic Controllers)
•	RTUs (Remote Terminal Units)
•	HMIs (Human-Machine Interfaces)
•	SCADA systems
•	Sensors, actuators, and robotics
 
Cyber-Physical Systems (CPS) integrate physical machinery with digital control, cloud connectivity, and enterprise systems. This hierarchy aligns with the Purdue Model: ICS is a subset of OT, and OT is a subset of CPS.
Real-world examples at Caterpillar include:
•	Welding robots
•	CNC machines
•	Assembly line automation
•	Telemetry-enabled heavy equipment
ICS devices differ from IT systems because they prioritize availability and safety over confidentiality, making them vulnerable to cyber threats if not properly segmented.
III. SYSTEM PERFORMANCE
System performance in an OT/ICS cybersecurity architecture refers to the ability of monitoring tools, segmentation controls, and incident response processes to detect, analyze, and respond to cyber threats across the Purdue Model’s layered environment.
A. Monitoring Across Purdue Levels
Security tools operate at different layers:
•	Microsoft Sentinel: SIEM visibility across Levels 4–5.
•	Microsoft Defender: Endpoint protection across IT systems.
•	Claroty / Nozomi Networks: Deep OT visibility across Levels 0–3.
•	ServiceNow: Incident tracking across all layers.
B. Integrated SOC Workflow
A typical investigation may involve:
1.	Sentinel detects anomalous login activity.
2.	Defender identifies suspicious process execution.
3.	Claroty/Nozomi detect abnormal PLC communication.
4.	ServiceNow coordinates escalation to IT and OT teams.
This integrated workflow ensures rapid detection and response.
C. Performance Metrics
Key performance indicators include:
•	Mean Time to Detect (MTTD)
•	Mean Time to Respond (MTTR)
•	False positive rate
•	OT protocol coverage
•	Segmentation effectiveness
•	Network visibility
High-performing systems provide rapid detection, accurate classification, and clear visibility across all Purdue levels.
D. Packet-Level Analysis Using Wireshark
Packet-level analysis is essential for validating alerts and understanding network behavior. Wireshark enables analysts to inspect:
•	DNS queries
•	TCP handshakes
•	HTTP requests
•	SMB traffic
•	ICS protocol communication
Wireshark supports investigations initiated by Sentinel, Defender, Claroty, or Nozomi by providing granular visibility into suspicious activity. It is particularly valuable for detecting early indicators of compromise such as abnormal PLC polling, unauthorized cross-level communication, or repeated DNS failures associated with malware.
